1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,498
A doctor reads a thermometer.

2
00:00:02,498 --> 00:00:04,444
A shopkeeper reads a scale.

3
00:00:04,444 --> 00:00:06,441
A student reads a stopwatch.

4
00:00:06,791 --> 00:00:09,470
None of them can read
the exact true value.

5
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:11,293
Every reading has some doubt.

6
00:00:11,743 --> 00:00:16,789
Diya divides the mass of some water by
its volume, and her calculator shows

7
00:00:16,789 --> 00:00:17,875
eight digits.

8
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:21,482
But her measuring jar was
only good to one millilitre.

9
00:00:21,832 --> 00:00:24,785
So which digits are real,
and which are noise?

10
00:00:24,785 --> 00:00:25,831
Let's find out.

11
00:00:26,381 --> 00:00:28,533
So what is measurement uncertainty?

12
00:00:28,883 --> 00:00:32,604
It is the range in which the
true value most likely lies.

13
00:00:32,604 --> 00:00:35,166
We call it delta, the plus or minus.

14
00:00:35,516 --> 00:00:37,379
A full answer has three parts.

15
00:00:37,379 --> 00:00:40,231
The best value, the
uncertainty, and the unit.

16
00:00:40,831 --> 00:00:42,842
Uncertainty is not a mistake.

17
00:00:42,842 --> 00:00:46,624
Even a perfect student with
a perfect ruler still has it.

18
00:00:47,174 --> 00:00:48,967
Let's see where it comes from.

19
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:50,892
A pencil on our measuring bench.

20
00:00:51,242 --> 00:00:55,208
With a centimetre scale, the end
falls between seven and eight.

21
00:00:55,208 --> 00:00:57,354
The uncertainty is one centimetre.

22
00:00:57,704 --> 00:00:59,792
Switch to a millimetre scale.

23
00:00:59,792 --> 00:01:04,043
Now it reads seven point four,
plus or minus zero point one.

24
00:01:04,393 --> 00:01:06,404
Switch to a vernier calliper.

25
00:01:06,404 --> 00:01:10,337
Seven point four three, plus
or minus zero point zero one.

26
00:01:10,687 --> 00:01:12,989
Better instrument, smaller error bar.

27
00:01:12,989 --> 00:01:14,886
But it never shrinks to zero.

28
00:01:15,436 --> 00:01:16,833
Look at the graph.

29
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:20,651
Each finer instrument gives
the answer one more real digit.

30
00:01:21,201 --> 00:01:22,971
Now, repeated readings.

31
00:01:22,971 --> 00:01:26,383
Diya times one swing of
a pendulum, five times.

32
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:31,437
Two point six two, two point five
eight, two point six four, two point

33
00:01:31,437 --> 00:01:33,554
six zero, two point six one.

34
00:01:33,904 --> 00:01:35,769
The best value is the mean.

35
00:01:35,769 --> 00:01:37,530
Two point six one seconds.

36
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:41,439
Each reading's distance from
the mean is its absolute error.

37
00:01:41,789 --> 00:01:45,092
The mean of those distances
is the uncertainty.

38
00:01:45,092 --> 00:01:49,564
Zero point zero one six, so about
zero point zero two seconds.

39
00:01:50,114 --> 00:01:52,647
Let's write the rules, one step at a time.

40
00:01:52,997 --> 00:01:53,897
Step one.

41
00:01:53,799 --> 00:01:55,716
The best value is the mean.

42
00:01:55,716 --> 00:01:59,198
a bar equals the sum of
the readings, divided by n.

43
00:01:59,848 --> 00:02:00,754
Step two.

44
00:02:00,754 --> 00:02:03,869
The uncertainty is the
mean absolute error.

45
00:02:03,869 --> 00:02:05,007
Delta a bar.

46
00:02:05,657 --> 00:02:06,674
Step three.

47
00:02:06,674 --> 00:02:10,783
Write the result as a bar,
plus or minus delta a bar.

48
00:02:10,783 --> 00:02:15,678
Here, two point six one plus or
minus zero point zero two seconds.

49
00:02:16,328 --> 00:02:17,404
Remember this.

50
00:02:17,404 --> 00:02:22,375
Every result is a best value, plus or
minus its uncertainty, with a unit.

51
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:24,242
Side by side.

52
00:02:24,242 --> 00:02:27,398
Diya's density, as her
calculator showed it.

53
00:02:27,398 --> 00:02:30,629
One point zero zero
five seven six nine two.

54
00:02:30,979 --> 00:02:33,021
And as a physicist writes it.

55
00:02:33,021 --> 00:02:36,669
Round the uncertainty, usually
to one significant figure.

56
00:02:36,669 --> 00:02:39,682
Then cut the value at
the same decimal place.

57
00:02:40,282 --> 00:02:41,396
Now you try.

58
00:02:41,396 --> 00:02:43,387
Which is written correctly?

59
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:48,525
Nine point eight two six plus or minus
zero point one, or nine point eight

60
00:02:48,525 --> 00:02:50,754
plus or minus zero point one?

61
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:52,139
Pause and decide.

62
00:02:55,339 --> 00:02:56,437
The second one!

63
00:02:56,437 --> 00:03:00,610
The uncertainty sits in the first
decimal place, so the value must stop

64
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:01,510
there too.

65
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:03,098
One more idea.

66
00:03:03,098 --> 00:03:07,402
To compare how good two results
are, use the relative uncertainty.

67
00:03:07,752 --> 00:03:12,813
A stopwatch gives two point six one,
plus or minus zero point zero two

68
00:03:12,813 --> 00:03:13,713
seconds.

69
00:03:13,613 --> 00:03:15,112
Under one percent.

70
00:03:15,462 --> 00:03:19,871
A wall clock gives two point six,
plus or minus zero point two.

71
00:03:19,871 --> 00:03:21,344
About eight percent.

72
00:03:21,694 --> 00:03:25,339
Same swing, but the stopwatch
result is ten times better.

73
00:03:25,889 --> 00:03:26,836
Your turn.

74
00:03:26,836 --> 00:03:31,200
A ruler gives ten centimetres,
plus or minus zero point one.

75
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:35,955
A tape gives one metre, plus or
minus zero point five centimetres.

76
00:03:35,955 --> 00:03:37,294
Which is better?

77
00:03:37,294 --> 00:03:38,687
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:41,887 --> 00:03:42,787
The tape!

79
00:03:42,656 --> 00:03:46,682
Its error is bigger, but it is
only half a percent of a metre.

80
00:03:46,682 --> 00:03:48,418
The ruler's is one percent.

81
00:03:48,968 --> 00:03:50,704
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:51,054 --> 00:03:56,133
A wire's length is measured four times:
fifty point two, fifty point five,

83
00:03:56,133 --> 00:03:59,501
fifty point three, fifty
point four centimetres.

84
00:03:59,951 --> 00:04:04,546
The mean is the sum, two hundred
and one point four, divided by four.

85
00:04:04,546 --> 00:04:06,136
Fifty point three five.

86
00:04:06,786 --> 00:04:12,131
The distances from the mean are zero
point one five, zero point one five,

87
00:04:12,131 --> 00:04:15,492
zero point zero five,
zero point zero five.

88
00:04:16,142 --> 00:04:18,212
Their mean is zero point one.

89
00:04:18,212 --> 00:04:19,910
That is the uncertainty.

90
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:25,117
The uncertainty is in the first decimal,
so round the value to fifty point

91
00:04:25,117 --> 00:04:26,017
four.

92
00:04:26,311 --> 00:04:31,283
The answer: fifty point four, plus
or minus zero point one centimetres.

93
00:04:31,833 --> 00:04:33,298
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:33,648 --> 00:04:37,421
Many students copy every
calculator digit into the answer.

95
00:04:37,771 --> 00:04:41,237
But a value can never be more
exact than its uncertainty.

96
00:04:41,237 --> 00:04:44,500
Round the uncertainty, then
round the value to match.

97
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:46,506
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:46,506 --> 00:04:50,766
As mean absolute error numericals,
and as picking the correctly written

99
00:04:50,766 --> 00:04:51,666
result.

100
00:04:52,011 --> 00:04:53,267
Exam level now.

101
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:55,662
Which result is written correctly?

102
00:04:55,662 --> 00:04:56,972
Pause and solve.

103
00:05:02,372 --> 00:05:03,748
The answer is B.

104
00:05:04,198 --> 00:05:08,797
The mean is eight point six two four,
and the mean absolute error is zero

105
00:05:08,797 --> 00:05:10,608
point zero two four eight.

106
00:05:11,058 --> 00:05:16,139
Rounded to one significant figure, the
uncertainty is zero point zero two.

107
00:05:16,139 --> 00:05:18,547
That is the second decimal place.

108
00:05:18,997 --> 00:05:21,050
So the value stops there too.

109
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:24,798
Eight point six two, plus or
minus zero point zero two.

110
00:05:25,398 --> 00:05:26,860
Here is your recap.

111
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:28,363
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:31,563 --> 00:05:34,561
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:05:34,911 --> 00:05:35,811
One.

114
00:05:35,453 --> 00:05:38,557
Why can no measurement
have zero uncertainty?

115
00:05:39,457 --> 00:05:40,357
Two.

116
00:05:39,964 --> 00:05:43,484
How do you find the uncertainty
from repeated readings?

117
00:05:44,384 --> 00:05:45,284
Three.

118
00:05:45,029 --> 00:05:50,315
Why is nine point eight two six plus or
minus zero point one written wrongly?

119
00:05:51,415 --> 00:05:53,620
That completes units and measurement.

120
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:57,405
Next: sitting still on a moving
bus, are you moving or not?

121
00:05:57,405 --> 00:05:58,526
Reference frames.
